<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Habitat of Wildlife – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Wildlife</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> The Habitat of Wildlife</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Contextualize basic classroom instructions supported with visual aids. Communication: Facilitate discussions on classroom rules.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can contextualize classroom instructions using visual aids (e.g., shows a picture of a jaguar for the instruction, "Choose your favorite animal."). Can facilitate a discussion on classroom rules by using simple gestures (e.g., making a "quiet" gesture for "no talking"). Can support instructions with visual aids to help peers understand (e.g., points to a picture and says, "This is the jaguar's habitat. You have to write about it.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of Panamanian animals (jaguar, sloth, harpy eagle) and their habitats (forest, jungle). Students quickly call out the animal and its habitat. Then, the teacher holds up an instruction card, for example, "Describe the jaguar's habitat," and asks students to use their hands to describe the animal, or point to a picture of a forest while saying the instruction. This activates prior knowledge and introduces the concept of supporting instructions with visuals/gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to contextualize instructions using visuals and gestures. For example, the teacher displays an image of a 'jaguar' and a 'forest'. The teacher says, "Listen: 'Jaguars live in the forest.' Now, how can I help someone understand 'forest'?" The teacher then points to the forest image and makes a gesture of trees. The teacher then models how to facilitate a discussion, using simple gestures like raising a hand for "who wants to speak" or a "quiet" gesture. The teacher also explicitly practices the pronunciation of long and short vowel sounds in words like "snake" (/sneɪk/) and "frog" (/frɒg/), highlighting the differences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'protect the habitat,' and show a picture of a clean forest, what do I mean?" (Elicit: "Keep it safe," "Don't destroy it.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I make this gesture (teacher makes a 'quiet' gesture), what classroom rule am I facilitating?" (Elicit: "No talking," "Be quiet.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Wildlife")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short set of instructions for the "Wildlife Documentary Storyboard" project, displayed on a projector or large poster, accompanied by corresponding images. For example:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 1: "Choose your favorite animal from Panama." (Visual: Pictures of Panamanian animals)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 2: "Describe its habitat." (Visual: Picture of a forest/jungle)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 3: "Use comparatives and superlatives." (Visual: Two animals with "bigger" or "faster" written, and one animal with "the biggest" or "the fastest")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruction 4: "Explain its habits using present simple." (Visual: A sloth hanging slowly from a branch)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Facilitate group discussions about environmental topics and follow instructions in collaborative activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the first step for our project?" (Gist question: Elicit "Choose an animal.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to Instruction 3. "Are we comparing two animals or just describing one?" (Elicit: "Comparing animals.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice contextualizing instructions and facilitating understanding using visuals, gestures, and target language. Focus on comparative and superlative adjectives, "there is/there are," present simple, and vocabulary (habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, predator, prey, species, endangered, protect, live, grow, hunt, survive, adapt).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Instruction-Visual Match & Explain (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes cards with basic instructions (e.g., "Write about its habitat," "Identify the fastest animal," "Protect biodiversity") and separate cards with simple visuals or gestures. In pairs, students match each instruction to the most helpful visual/gesture. Then, one student explains to their partner, "This picture (points) helps us understand 'habitat' because it shows where animals live." They also practice syllable division for compound nouns like "wildlife" (wild-life) and "biodiversity" (bio-di-ver-si-ty) as they explain the terms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Explain the Rule" Chain (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher gives the first student a simple instruction related to the storyboard project, e.g., "Mention three facts about the jaguar." The student must explain this instruction to the next student using one visual aid (drawing, flashcard) or a gesture, and using present simple (e.g., "The jaguar lives in the forest. It hunts prey."). The next student then repeats the instruction and explains it to the following student, adding a new element or rephrasing using vocabulary like "endangered" or "resilient."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Classroom Rules (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs to discuss and 'mediate' a few classroom rules for working on the storyboard project. They must agree on 2-3 rules (e.g., "No talking loudly," "Share materials," "Help your partner"). For each rule, they create a simple visual or gesture to help explain it. One pair presents their rules and visuals to another pair, mediating the meaning to ensure understanding. They might say, "This gesture (finger to lips) means 'no talking.' It helps us protect a quiet 'habitat' for thinking."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Panama's Wildlife")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Presentation / Storyboard Explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students, in small groups, present sections of their "Wildlife Documentary Storyboard." Their main task is to *mediate* their storyboard's content and the instructions for creating it, to a peer group or the class. Each group member takes turns explaining a part of their storyboard (e.g., animal, habitat, behavior, comparison to another animal), ensuring their audience understands by using visual aids (their storyboard drawings/pictures), gestures, and clear, simple language. They must incorporate the target grammar (present simple, comparatives/superlatives like "The harpy eagle is stronger than the hawk," "The sloth moves slowly") and vocabulary (habitat, endangered, nocturnal, protect, adapt). One student in each group must act as a facilitator, using learned techniques (gestures, simple questions) to encourage discussion and clarify points during a Q&A. For example, a student might point to an image of a jaguar in the forest and say, "This is the jaguar. It lives in the jungle. It is bigger than a ocelot." They would then ask, "Do you understand 'jungle'?" and point to the visual for clarification if needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist with a focus on effective mediation strategies and peer assessment using a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents a 2-3 minute excerpt from their "Wildlife Documentary Storyboard" to another group. They must effectively mediate information about their chosen Panamanian animal and its habitat, explaining key facts, behaviors, and comparisons. One member facilitates a brief peer Q&A session, ensuring classmates understand the presented content. For example, a student points to a drawing of a sloth and explains, "This is a sloth. It moves slowly. There are many sloths in the forest. It is slower than a monkey." Then they might use a gesture for "question" to prompt peer engagement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary (e.g., long and short vowel sounds in "eagle," "bat," correct syllable division for "wildlife").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Consistent use of target vocabulary (habitat, jaguar, forest, endangered, protect, live, adapt, species).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear contextualization of instructions/information using visual aids and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective facilitation of group discussions and collaborative activities (e.g., asking clarifying questions, encouraging participation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of comparative/superlative adjectives and 'there is/there are' to describe animals and habitats.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One way I helped my classmates understand my storyboard today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or grammar point I used when explaining was..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining your storyboard did you find the most challenging, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using visuals or gestures help your classmates understand your message?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate information and facilitate discussions today? (e.g., specific visuals, pairing strategies, modeling of gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for students to better contextualize instructions or lead discussions in future lessons? (e.g., more practice with specific grammar structures, explicit training in discussion facilitation techniques).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "The Habitat of Wildlife" and the "Wildlife Documentary Storyboard" project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of linguistic objectives, building on the communicative skills developed in this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>